InvalidMinutes is a custom error type. Deriving Debug supports diagnostic formatting; implementing Display supplies the user-facing message. The fmt method borrows a Formatter destination; its underscore lifetime placeholder is inferred. fmt::Result is the formatting Result type, and write! returns that Result. Implementing std::error::Error marks it as a standard error type. It does not automatically add recovery or validation.

This smaller formatting companion actually requests Display through the {} placeholder. fmt receives a shared error receiver and a borrowed mutable Formatter; write! sends the supplied message to that destination and returns the formatting Result, which fmt returns unchanged. It does not print independently of the caller.
```rust
use std::fmt;
#[derive(Debug)]
struct InvalidMinutes;
impl fmt::Display for InvalidMinutes {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "minutes must be 1 through 60")
    }
}
impl std::error::Error for InvalidMinutes {}
fn main() {
    println!("{}", InvalidMinutes);
}
```
Expected output: minutes must be 1 through 60.

